@database utf8.guide
@$VER: utf8.guide 0.1b2 (17.10.2026)
@author Tobias Karlsson

@node main "utf8.library"

@{b}utf8.library programmer's guide@{ub}

(Other scripts are spelt in Latin letters here, as the Amiga shows them;
docs/guide.md and docs/reference.md have the original characters.)

utf8.library lets an Amiga program work with @{b}UTF-8@{ub}, the text of the web,
of mail, of every modern computer, while the Amiga itself speaks @{b}Latin-1@{ub}.
It counts, measures, cuts, searches, compares and converts UTF-8 text, and
turns it into and out of Latin-1 and thirteen other 8-bit sets. It works
from C (and any language that can call a library) and from @{b}ARexx@{ub}.

This guide explains how to use it. Every call is described in full in the
@{"reference" link "reference"}. Complete working programs: tests/first.c (this guide's first
program), tests/utf8test.c (every call, C) and tests/utf8test.rexx
(ARexx).

@{b}The programmer's guide@{ub}

  @{"What you need" link "g-what-you-need"}
  @{"The first program" link "g-the-first-program"}
  @{"How text is passed" link "g-how-text-is-passed"}
  @{"Bytes, code points, characters, columns" link "g-bytes-code-points-characters-columns"}
  @{"Text in a terminal" link "g-text-in-a-terminal"}
  @{"An editor's cursor" link "g-an-editors-cursor"}
  @{"Case, accents, searching and sorting" link "g-case-accents-searching-and-sorting"}
  @{"Character sets" link "g-character-sets"}
